92	/// Keyed by a hash of the file's actual byte content, not `(size, mtime)`.
93	///
94	/// The prior mtime+size scheme had a documented staleness gap (P0-2): on
95	/// filesystems/environments with coarse mtime resolution (network FS,
96	/// container overlay FS, or simply two edits landing in the same clock
97	/// tick), an edit completing within the same tick and producing a
98	/// same-length file was indistinguishable from the original — a stale
99	/// `SourceData` was served with no signal anything was wrong. A content hash
100	/// closes this gap exactly: two different byte sequences essentially never
101	/// hash to the same 64-bit `FxHasher` value (birthday-bound collision risk
102	/// is negligible at this cache's `MAX_CACHE_ENTRIES` scale), and identical
103	/// bytes always hash identically regardless of mtime/clock granularity.
104	/// This trades away the old stat-only check's near-zero I/O cost — computing
105	/// the hash requires the file's content, not just its metadata — but every
106	/// call site already reads the full file on a miss to parse it anyway; `get`
107	/// and `insert` both take that same content as a parameter instead of
108	/// re-deriving a (now-unreliable) proxy for it, so no call site pays for an
109	/// extra read this fix didn't already need.
